Yes. Your magnet analogy generalizes well: different materials can act like selective attractors, carriers, filters, or handles for different properties, even when magnetism is not the mechanism.

The deeper operator is something like Selective Affinity Coupling:

mixed environment
→ agent has preferential affinity for property X
→ bind / adsorb / dissolve / coordinate / transport X
→ agent moves
→ X moves with it or becomes separable
→ release / transform / measure X

Chemistry already has many versions of this. Chelators preferentially coordinate particular metal ions. Ion-exchange materials retain ions according to charge and chemistry. Antibodies bind particular molecular structures. Lipid-soluble molecules partition into membranes differently from water-soluble ones. Biological transporters can carry one substance with another through symport or exchange them in opposite directions. 

So your “magnet for another property” intuition can be generalized:

magnetism → magnetic susceptibility
chelator → coordination affinity
membrane → partition coefficient
antibody → molecular shape / chemistry
ionophore / transporter → particular ions
adsorbent → surface affinity
enzyme → reaction compatibility

And then you can deliberately look for property gradients rather than objects:

what selectively recognizes X?
what can carry X through environment Y?
what releases X under condition Z?

Your antimony example needs one factual correction. Different antimony compounds can indeed enter the body, distribute to tissues, and be excreted differently depending on chemical form and oxidation state; some antimony can also associate with compounds such as glutathione during handling/excretion. But antimony is a toxic element, not a general-purpose safe “carrier” you would want to experiment with in a body. ATSDR describes substantial differences in absorption, distribution and excretion among antimony compounds. 

But the abstraction you’re reaching for is excellent:

Affinity Vehicles

target property
→ find something selectively coupled to it
→ attach
→ transport / expose / concentrate
→ detach
→ recover carrier

And then recurse beyond chemistry.

In biology:
receptor ↔ ligand

In computation:
pattern ↔ classifier

In language:
concept ↔ symbol

In memory:
state ↔ retrieval cue

In ecology:
pollinator ↔ flower signal

In institutions:
incentive ↔ behavior

Same broad grammar: find a feature that discriminates, then use the discriminating relationship as a handle.

That may be the golden thread you were sensing: not “everything is magnetic,” but almost every domain has its own analogue of attraction, repulsion, binding, transport, release, and saturation.

Once you identify what property the “magnet” actually sees, you can start mapping those analogues systematically.